By your mid-20s, if you're lucky enough to have a job, it's time to move out of the house and support yourself. You've saved some money. Your prospects are decent. Success isn't guaranteed, but you can't live on mommy and daddy's money forever, right?
That's how it feels with this economy, and why it's time for the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates. The country is more than five years past the worst economic crisis since the 1930s, still recovering but strong enough to get by on its own. Friday's strong jobs report adds to the evidence. Time for the Fed to cut the cord and allow the economy to function, and grow, without the unnatural support of super-low rates.
The next meeting of the Fed is in mid-March but, unfortunately, that's not when a rate rise is expected. Fed officials say an increase won't be on the table until the June or September meetings, and even that's no sure thing. A prevailing school of thought says the worst action would be to raise rates too quickly, hindering a recovery that's still uneven. By this thinking, it would be better to wait until late this year or even 2016, giving the economy all the juice possible to goose growth and get more people working.
We beg to differ. Here's why:
When you look at the numbers, look around the country, and consider your own financial well-being, you'll probably conclude that things are better — and your fellow Americans feel more secure. True, the country isn't where it should be, but progress is undeniable. A lot of people have gone back to work: The unemployment rate has fallen from a high of 10 percent in 2009 to 5.5 percent in Friday's federal unemployment report. The economy's growing, albeit slowly. Housing prices are rising, at a sensible pace, and the stock market's zooming. Inflation is very low, too, even beyond the bargain prices at the gas pump.
Yet Janet Yellen, the Fed chief, and others see a lot to be desired. "Too many Americans remain unemployed or underemployed, wage growth is still sluggish and inflation remains well below our longer-run objective," she told Congress last month. She's right: Millions of people who have part-time jobs want full-time work; millions more have given up looking and bailed out of the job market altogether. If you have a job, you probably haven't had a raise in quite a while, unless you work in one of a few lucky fields such as accounting or engineering.
The rest of the world is struggling, too. China's growth has hit a wall, Japan is in recession again and Europe is still trying to right its ship. Central banks in all three regions are loosening the strings to jolt growth. There really isn't any reason for the Fed to pull away from the pack and end the party here, at the risk of undermining the U.S. recovery.
Except that it's the right thing to do.
The Fed's mission is to manage growth, keeping an eye on the labor market and inflation. Its primary mechanism is control of interest rates. And the Fed's benchmark rate is near zero. Predicting future prospects is tricky, yes, but like Wayne Gretzky said, you skate to where the puck will be, not where it's been.
With momentum in the economy positive, there would be considerable lag time between the Fed's actions and any real-world discomfort. Super-low interest rates are intended to entice businesses to go to banks and borrow money to grow, encourage new business creation, and drive consumers to borrow and spend. Rates will still be low after the Fed acts, but that won{r}'t{r} make{r} the{r} economy{r} more{r} resilient{r}.{r} That{r}'s{r} why{r} it{r}'s{r} important{r} to{r} know{r} about{r} policy{r} and{r} its{r} underlying{r} consequences{g}.{r} And{r} whether{r} it{r}'s{r} effective{r} will{g} be{r} important{r} to{r} know{r} for{r} sure{r}.{r}
{r}So{r},{r} for{r} now{r},{r} the{r} best{r} course{r} to{r} take{r} is{r} to{r} stay{r} in{r} the{r} job{r} market{r}.{r}
